# Modeling count data: Poisson and negative binomial {#sec-count-regression}

















@sec-glm-intro ended with a negative binomial model that finally behaved, and with two promises I made in passing. The first was about the counting window. I told you I was treating the region of interest as the same size for every animal, and that the fix for the day it isn't (an *offset*) would wait for this chapter. The second was about zeros: the PDGFR-β counts of that chapter never dropped below about ninety cells, which is a luxury. Spike counts, and most counts you'll collect in an electrophysiology rig, are not so kind.

I'll say something about how the chapter is put together, because it changes scene halfway. We start on the histology counts you know, and go back to the negative binomial to read the one number, `shape`, that I let you skip over last time. Then we give those counts what they've been missing, a denominator. And then we leave the microscope for the rig, where a new kind of zero appears and the negative binomial, on its own, can't explain it.

## What `shape` is really telling us

Last chapter I reported the `shape` of the negative binomial, said it was "small enough that the extra variance is substantial", and moved on. That was a bit like handing you a thermometer reading without saying which way is hot. So before we add anything, let me reopen that fit, without refitting it, and read it properly.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# The negative binomial of @sec-glm-intro, loaded from its cache (not refit)
fit_nb <- readRDS("models/glm-intro_fit3.rds")
shape_draws <- as.data.frame(posterior::as_draws_df(fit_nb))$shape
```
:::








Here is the way I like to think about `shape`. Imagine two pericyte-rich animals on the same day after ischemia. Even before you put a section under the microscope, one of them simply has more PDGFR-β+ cells in that territory than the other, for reasons that are biological and not your fault. The negative binomial says each animal has its *own* true density, and that those densities scatter around the day's mean by a multiplicative wobble. `shape` (which I'll write $\phi$, as in the previous chapter) is the dial that controls how big that wobble is: the animal-to-animal coefficient of variation of the true rate is $1/\sqrt{\phi}$ [@inchausti2023]. A large $\phi$ means the animals are almost interchangeable; as $\phi \to \infty$ the wobble vanishes and you're left with the Poisson. A small $\phi$ means animals are individuals.

For our fit, `shape` has a posterior median of 4.8, so before we have counted a single cell, animals of the same day already differ by about 46% in their true PDGFR-β+ density. That figure answers a question that comes up at the bench all the time: *if I count more cells per animal, will the noise go away?* The total variance of a count is $\mu + \mu^2/\phi$, so its coefficient of variation, the standard deviation relative to the mean, is

$$
\text{CV}(y) = \sqrt{\frac{1}{\mu} + \frac{1}{\phi}}
$$ {#eq-cv-nb}

The first term is the counting noise: it shrinks as the count grows. The second is the animal-to-animal noise: it doesn't depend on the count at all. I'll draw both against each other, with the Poisson (which has only the first term) for contrast, and the observed CV of each day's animals as points. I'm using the CV, not the variance as in @sec-glm-intro, because the CV is on the scale a scientist thinks in ("the counts wobble by 40% or so"), and it turns the two noise terms into a picture: one keeps falling, the other is a floor.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
mu_grid <- exp(seq(log(300), log(4500), length.out = 80))
phi_sub <- shape_draws[seq(1, length(shape_draws), length.out = 400)]

cv_band <- data.frame(
  mu = mu_grid,
  lo = sapply(mu_grid, function(m) quantile(sqrt(1 / m + 1 / phi_sub), 0.025)),
  mid = sapply(mu_grid, function(m) median(sqrt(1 / m + 1 / phi_sub))),
  hi = sapply(mu_grid, function(m) quantile(sqrt(1 / m + 1 / phi_sub), 0.975)),
  pois = 1 / sqrt(mu_grid)
)

day_cv <- aggregate(Pdgfrb_Pos ~ DPI_f, data = cell_data,
                    FUN = function(x) c(m = mean(x), cv = sd(x) / mean(x)))
day_cv <- do.call(data.frame, day_cv)
names(day_cv) <- c("DPI_f", "m", "cv")

ggplot(cv_band, aes(x = mu)) +
  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = lo, ymax = hi), fill = "#0072B2", alpha = 0.25) +
  geom_line(aes(y = mid), color = "#0072B2", linewidth = 1) +
  geom_line(aes(y = pois), color = "#D55E00", linewidth = 1, linetype = "dashed") +
  geom_point(data = day_cv, aes(x = m, y = cv), size = 3) +
  ggrepel::geom_text_repel(data = day_cv, aes(x = m, y = cv, label = paste("DPI", DPI_f)),
                           size = 3, min.segment.length = 0.2) +
  annotate("text", x = 4700, y = cv_band$mid[80], label = "Negative
binomial",
           color = "#0072B2", hjust = 0, size = 3.5) +
  annotate("text", x = 4700, y = cv_band$pois[80], label = "Poisson",
           color = "#D55E00", hjust = 0, size = 3.5) +
  scale_x_log10(labels = scales::label_comma(), breaks = c(300, 1000, 3000),
                expand = expansion(mult = c(0.05, 0.3))) +
  scale_y_log10(breaks = c(0.03, 0.1, 0.3, 1), labels = scales::label_percent()) +
  labs(x = "Mean count", y = "Coefficient of variation") +
  Plot_theme
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Coefficient of variation of a count against its mean. The Poisson keeps falling as the count grows; the negative binomial levels off at a floor set by shape (band, 95% credible interval). Points are the observed CV of each day's animals.](count-regression_files/figure-html/fig-count_cv_floor-1.png){#fig-count_cv_floor width=624}
:::
:::








The Poisson (dashed) says that a group averaging four thousand cells should be pinned down to about 1.6%. The observed animals sit ten to thirty times higher. The negative binomial has a floor at $1/\sqrt{\phi}$ (roughly the 46% we computed), and the animals sit near it (day 14 a little below, which with a handful of animals per day is unsurprising). This is a fact about the *experiment*, not just about the model: past a point, a bigger ROI or a more patient counter buys almost nothing, because the remaining noise is between animals, and the only cure for that is more animals. If you have ever wondered why your reviewers ask for more mice when you already counted thousands of cells per section, this figure is the reason.

::: callout-tip
### Which `shape` is it? The same number under several names

`shape` in `brms` is the `theta` that `MASS::glm.nb()` prints, and in both the variance is $\mu + \mu^2/\text{shape}$; `glmmTMB`'s `nbinom2` family uses the same form. Other software parametrizes the same distribution with the *reciprocal*, $1/\phi$, so that zero means "Poisson" (a small number, then, is a good sign there, and a large number is a bad one). Whenever you read a paper that reports "the dispersion parameter", check which of the two it is before you decide whether the data are overdispersed.
:::

## "Per what?": the offset

Every scientist has had the following conversation. You report that treated animals have 1,900 PDGFR-β+ cells and the controls have 500. Somebody at the back asks: *per what?* A count is like a receipt with the shop's name torn off: it tells you how many, and it says nothing about how big the window was that you looked through. If sections differ in size, the count mixes two things: how dense the cells are, and how much tissue you counted.

`cell_data` does have that denominator. `Tissue_Area` is the area of the section, in mm², over which the cells were counted. (There's a second column, `Tisue_Area`, with a typo in its name: it is the very same measurement in µm², a million times bigger, so I won't use it.) I assumed in @sec-glm-intro that this area was the same for every animal. It isn't.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(cell_data, aes(x = DPI_f, y = Tissue_Area)) +
  geom_jitter(aes(color = Condition, shape = Condition),
              width = 0.12, size = 2.5, alpha = 0.85) +
  stat_summary(fun = mean, geom = "crossbar", width = 0.5,
               linewidth = 0.4, color = "black") +
  scale_color_manual(values = c(SHAM = "#D55E00", MCAO = "#0072B2")) +
  scale_y_continuous(limits = c(0, NA)) +
  labs(x = "Days post-ischemia (DPI)", y = "Counted tissue area (mm²)") +
  Plot_theme +
  theme(legend.position = "top")
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Area of the counted tissue for each animal, by day after ischemia. The area is not constant, and it shrinks as the days go by.](count-regression_files/figure-html/fig-count_area-1.png){#fig-count_area width=624}
:::
:::

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
area_by_day <- tapply(cell_data$Tissue_Area, cell_data$DPI_f, mean)
```
:::








The area falls from about 15 mm² in the sham animals to about 8 mm² at day 30, which is what you'd expect from tissue that loses volume as the infarct matures. Two consequences. The first is that counts are not comparable across animals as they stand. The second is subtler and I find it more interesting: the area is not noise that's independent of the question, it moves *with* the day, so it will change the answer.

### What an offset does

The idea is old and simple. If the counting window is $A_i$ and the true density of cells is $\theta_i$, then the expected count is the density times the window, $\mu_i = A_i \theta_i$. Take logs, and the window turns into something we can add:

$$
\log(\mu_i) = \log(A_i) + \log(\theta_i) = \log(A_i) + \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_{1i} + \dots
$$ {#eq-offset}

The term $\log(A_i)$ is called an **offset**. It is a predictor whose coefficient isn't estimated but fixed to exactly 1, which is how the model says "double the window, double the expected count" [@gelman2020; @inchausti2023; @mcelreath2020]. What remains on the right, $\beta_0 + \beta_1 x_1 + \dots$, is now a model for the *density*: cells per mm², not cells per section. The coefficients keep the interpretation they had before (log fold changes), but the thing being multiplied is a rate.

Before fitting I need priors on the log scale, and I'd like to keep them judged on the *outcome* scale, per the warning in @sec-glm-intro. With the offset, the `Intercept` is the log of the density (cells per mm²) of a day-0 animal with a typical section size. In @sec-glm-intro the day-0 mean was about 500 cells, over sections of about 14 mm², so around 35 cells/mm². I'll use `Normal(4, 1.5)`, which puts 95% of its mass between roughly 3 and 1,100 cells/mm², a range that comfortably includes what we saw and is far from "anything goes". For the day coefficients, `Normal(0, 1.5)` allows fold changes from about 1/20 to 20, wider than anything the biology here would deliver (the largest change we saw last time was under tenfold). I keep `brms`'s default for `shape`, an inverse-gamma(0.4, 0.3), which is weakly informative and keeps the parameter away from zero.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
prior_hist <- c(
  prior(normal(4, 1.5), class = "Intercept"),
  prior(normal(0, 1.5), class = "b"),
  prior(inv_gamma(0.4, 0.3), class = "shape")
)

fit_offset <- brm(
  Pdgfrb_Pos ~ DPI_f + offset(log(Tissue_Area)),
  data = cell_data,
  family = negbinomial(),
  prior = prior_hist,
  seed = 123,
  file = "models/count-regression_fit1.rds",
  file_refit = "never"
)
```
:::








