Sampling is healthy. The `Intercept` of 3.64 is $\log$(cells per mm²) on day 0, which is about 38 cells/mm², close to what I guessed from the raw numbers. And `shape` barely moved: 4.8, the same as before. The offset changed *what* is being modeled, but the animal-to-animal wobble, being a property of the animals, stayed where it was.

What did change is the story about the time course. Here are the fold changes relative to day 0 from the model of @sec-glm-intro (raw counts) and from this one (density), side by side.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
day_cols <- c("b_DPI_f3", "b_DPI_f7", "b_DPI_f14", "b_DPI_f30")
d_cnt <- as.data.frame(posterior::as_draws_df(fit_nb))[, day_cols]
d_dens <- as.data.frame(posterior::as_draws_df(fit_offset))[, day_cols]

fold <- rbind(
  data.frame(model = "Count per section", day = rep(day_cols, each = nrow(d_cnt)),
             fold = exp(unlist(d_cnt))),
  data.frame(model = "Density per mm²", day = rep(day_cols, each = nrow(d_dens)),
             fold = exp(unlist(d_dens)))
)
fold$day <- factor(sub("b_DPI_f", "", fold$day), levels = c(30, 14, 7, 3))

ggplot(fold, aes(x = fold, y = day, color = model, shape = model)) +
  stat_pointinterval(.width = c(0.5, 0.95), position = position_dodge(width = 0.5),
                     point_size = 2.5) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed", color = "grey40") +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Count per section" = "#D55E00", "Density per mm²" = "#0072B2")) +
  scale_x_log10(breaks = c(0.5, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16)) +
  labs(x = "Fold change vs. day 0 (log scale)", y = "Days post-ischemia") +
  Plot_theme +
  theme(legend.position = "top", legend.title = element_blank())
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Fold change relative to day 0 in PDGFR-β+ cells, as a raw count (model of @sec-glm-intro) and as a density per mm² (offset model). Points are posterior medians; thick and thin bars are 50% and 95% credible intervals.](count-regression_files/figure-html/fig-count_fold_offset-1.png){#fig-count_fold_offset width=624}
:::
:::

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
fc14_cnt <- exp(d_cnt$b_DPI_f14)
fc14_dens <- exp(d_dens$b_DPI_f14)
```
:::








At day 14, the number of PDGFR-β+ cells per section was about 5.8 times the day-0 number (5.8 [3.7, 9.0]); their density per mm² was about 8.4 times (8.4 [5.3, 12.7]). Both statements are true, and they answer different questions: *there are more cells in the section*, and *the tissue that's left is more crowded with them*. The second is bigger because the tissue shrank while the cells accumulated. If your hypothesis is about the density of pericytes in what remains of the tissue, which is what I'd usually want, then the raw count understates the effect at the later days (at day 7, where the area is close to the day-0 area, the two agree), in the same direction as the atrophy. That's the sense in which the offset is not a technicality: it changed the day-14 estimate by roughly 45%.

::: callout-note
### An offset is an assumption, not a discovery

The offset says the expected count is *exactly proportional* to the area. Nothing in the data forced that: it comes from how the counting was done. The textbook way to check it [@gelman2020; @mcelreath2020; @inchausti2023] is to put $\log(A_i)$ in the model as an ordinary predictor with a free coefficient and see whether 1 is a believable value for it. So let me do that.
:::








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
prior_free <- c(prior_hist, prior(normal(0, 1), class = "b", coef = "log_area"))

fit_free <- brm(
  Pdgfrb_Pos ~ DPI_f + log_area,
  data = cell_data,
  family = negbinomial(),
  prior = prior_free,
  seed = 123,
  file = "models/count-regression_fit2.rds",
  file_refit = "never"
)

b_area <- as.data.frame(posterior::as_draws_df(fit_free))$b_log_area
```
:::








The coefficient of log-area comes out at 0.06 [-0.96, 1.01]. The interval is wide enough to contain 1 (the value the offset imposes) and also 0 (the value that would say area doesn't matter at all). The data cannot tell those apart, and the reason is worth understanding: area and day are tangled up, since the sections get smaller as the days go by, and within a single day the areas are fairly similar. There isn't enough independent variation in area to learn what it does on its own. Comparing the two models by leave-one-out cross-validation says the same thing, that neither is predicting new animals better.








::: {#tbl-count_loo_area .cell tbl-cap='Leave-one-out comparison of the offset model (coefficient fixed at 1) and the model with a free coefficient for log-area'}

```{.r .cell-code}
loo_offset <- loo(fit_offset)
loo_free <- loo(fit_free)
cmp_area <- loo_compare(list("Offset (coefficient = 1)" = loo_offset, "Free coefficient" = loo_free))

cmp_area_tbl <- data.frame(
  Model = rownames(cmp_area),
  `elpd difference` = round(cmp_area[, "elpd_diff"], 1),
  `SE of difference` = round(cmp_area[, "se_diff"], 1),
  check.names = FALSE
)

gt(cmp_area_tbl)
```

