That is not a failure of the offset; it's what the exposure in your design is *for*. In an experiment where I know that the count is taken over a window of a certain size, I don't need the data to tell me that the count is proportional to that window. I need them to not contradict me, and here they don't. What I would not do is claim the data *confirmed* the offset. Use it because of the design, check the free coefficient when the data can speak to it (with a lot of variation in exposure at each level of the predictors, this check gets sharp), and say which of those you did.

## A new scene: spikes

Now to the second promise. Take the histology hat off and put on the electrophysiologist's. Suppose you record 150 single units from cortex, half from control animals and half from animals given a drug that is supposed to quiet spontaneous activity. For each unit you count the spikes emitted in a window of spontaneous activity. **Does the drug reduce firing?**

::: callout-note
### These data are simulated, on purpose

As in @sec-logistic-regression, I don't have a dataset with the structure I need, so I generated one, in `functions/simulate_ephys.R`, with a fixed seed. The recording window of each unit differs in length (20 to 120 seconds, because a recording is cut when the unit stops being stable), which is the exposure of this chapter; and, as in real recordings, the file has one row per unit. In real experiments the units come from a handful of animals, so they are not independent of each other; I treat them as independent here to keep the chapter about counts, and @sec-multilevel-glmm is where that shortcut gets repaired.
:::

Let me look at all the units first. A spike count against the length of the recording is the honest view, because it shows the exposure and the outcome together.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(spikes, aes(x = duration_s, y = spikes, color = group, shape = group)) +
  geom_point(size = 2.3, alpha = 0.8) +
  scale_color_manual(values = group_cols) +
  labs(x = "Recording length (s)", y = "Spikes in the window") +
  Plot_theme +
  theme(legend.position = "top", legend.title = element_blank())
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Spikes emitted by each unit against the length of its recording, by group. The units at the bottom, on the zero line, emitted nothing.](count-regression_files/figure-html/fig-count_spikes_raw-1.png){#fig-count_spikes_raw width=624}
:::
:::

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
zero_share <- tapply(spikes$spikes == 0, spikes$group, mean)
```
:::








Two features stand out. The counts fan out as the recordings get longer, as they should if firing is a rate: longer window, more spikes, and more room to differ. And there is a suspicious floor: 26% of the units have zero spikes, 19% of the controls and 33% of the treated units. Hold that thought. First, do what you'd do with any new count: use the model we already trust, with the offset that the recording length calls for.

### The negative binomial, with an offset

Duration is the exposure, so the offset is $\log(\text{duration}_i)$, and the model is for the firing rate in spikes per second. Priors again on the outcome scale. The `Intercept` is the log firing rate of a control unit in Hz, and `Normal(-1.5, 1.5)` puts 95% of its mass between about 0.01 and 4.5 spikes/s, the range in which the spontaneous firing of cortical neurons plausibly lives. The group coefficient is a log rate ratio: `Normal(0, 0.7)` allows rate ratios from about 0.25 to 4, which is more than any single drug should manage. The `shape` keeps its default.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
prior_spk <- c(
  prior(normal(-1.5, 1.5), class = "Intercept"),
  prior(normal(0, 0.7), class = "b"),
  prior(inv_gamma(0.4, 0.3), class = "shape")
)

fit_nb_spk <- brm(
  spikes ~ group + offset(log(duration_s)),
  data = spikes,
  family = negbinomial(),
  prior = prior_spk,
  init = 0,
  control = list(adapt_delta = 0.95),
  seed = 123,
  file = "models/count-regression_fit3.rds",
  file_refit = "never"
)
```
:::








::: callout-warning
### When one chain wanders off

With `brms`'s default random starting values, this model was hit by a chain that drifted into a degenerate corner of parameter space, where the counts are "explained" by a vanishing mean and an astronomically large `shape`, and never came back. The summary showed an `Rhat` of 3 and hundreds of divergent transitions, which is the sign the chapter on MCMC taught you to respect: don't read that fit. Starting every chain at 0 on the link scale (`init = 0`, a rate of one spike per second, not a bad guess) and a slightly higher `adapt_delta` fixed it, and I report the fix instead of hiding it because the same thing can happen to you on data with many zeros, and the cure isn't to "run it longer".
:::

