The fit is unremarkable at first sight: the treated units fire at 0.54 [0.36, 0.81] times the control rate. But look at `shape`: 0.65. In the histology counts it was about five; here it is below one, and by @eq-cv-nb the animal-to-animal wobble of the true rate is about 124%, over a hundred percent. Either these neurons really are wildly different from one another, or the model is using `shape` to absorb something it has no other way to say. The posterior predictive check tells us which. For counts, the right check isn't the density overlay of the earlier chapters: with discrete data and a spike at zero, a kernel density smooths away the one thing we care about. A **rootogram** is designed for this [@gelman2026]. It compares, for each possible count, how many units the model expects with how many we saw, on a square-root scale so that the rare high counts remain visible. In the "hanging" style, the bars hang from the model's expected curve down toward the zero line: a bar that stops short of the line means the model expected more units of that count than we saw; one that crosses it means the model expected fewer. I add the simplest zero-specific check next to it, the share of zeros in replicated data against the observed share.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
zeros <- function(x) mean(x == 0)

ppc_pair <- function(fit) {
  p1 <- pp_check(fit, type = "rootogram", style = "hanging", prob = 0.95) +
    coord_cartesian(xlim = c(0, 40)) +
    labs(title = "Rootogram", x = "Spikes in the window")
  p2 <- pp_check(fit, type = "stat", stat = "zeros", ndraws = 500) +
    labs(title = "Share of zeros", x = "Proportion of units with 0 spikes")
  p1 + p2 + plot_layout(widths = c(1.4, 1))
}
```
:::

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ppc_pair(fit_nb_spk)
```

