All seven pass: the worst Rhat is 1.003, the smallest effective sample size is 1818, and there are 0 divergent transitions in total. The spike-count model that misbehaved with default starting values is now among the cleanest, which is the whole argument for fixing the start instead of running the chains longer.

## Where this leaves us

We started with two loose ends from the previous chapters and they turned out to be the same kind of lesson. The `shape` of a negative binomial is a statement about how different the *units* are from each other before you count anything, and it sets a floor on the noise that counting more cannot lower, which is a statement about your experimental design. An **offset** gives a count its denominator: it fixes the coefficient of $\log(\text{exposure})$ to 1, turns the model into one for a rate, and, in the histology data, changed the size of the time course because the tissue shrank while the cells piled up. It's an assumption of the design, and the data could not tell us whether it was right, so I used it for the reason that we counted over a known window. And when a count has more zeros than any negative binomial can honestly explain, the honest model separates two kinds of silence, the neurons that can't fire from the ones that didn't, and lets you ask two questions of a drug instead of one.

One shortcut runs through the whole chapter, and I've flagged it twice: every unit and every animal was treated as independent of the others. Real units come from a few animals, and the count of an animal's units is not the same as the number of independent pieces of evidence. @sec-multilevel-glmm is the chapter that repairs it. Before that, @sec-beta-robust-regression turns to outcomes that are continuous but bounded (proportions of time in a zone) or heavy-tailed (reaction times), the last two families of this part.
